# Commit messages

Write messages for a future reader and the reviewer. The diff shows what changed. The message should make the decision make sense months later.

## Subject

```
type(scope): imperative description
```

- Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`, `perf`, `build`, `ci`
- Scope names the area (`readme`, `rules`, `comparison`, `web`, `position`)
- Imperative, lowercase after the colon ("point", "stop", "add")
- No trailing period
- Keep the full subject under 72 characters

## Body

- Blank line after the subject
- Wrap at 72 characters
- Explain why. Say what was wrong before, and what to use instead if a file or command was removed
- Skip the body only when the subject is the whole story, such as a typo fix

## Example

```
docs(comparison): point lap overlay at the visualizer API

compare_laps.py is gone. The overlay is
InteractiveTelemetryVisualizer.plot_lap_comparison().

The old CLI examples would send a reader to a file that does not
exist. Position-based comparison remains the better view when
track_position is present.
```

One logical change per commit. Pass the message with a HEREDOC so the blank line and wrapping are preserved.
